19:T5fb,## Two rules

The **D** in SOLID:

1. **High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules.** Both should depend on **abstractions**.
2. **Abstractions should not depend on details.** Details should depend on abstractions.

“High-level” = the policy/business logic (`NotificationService`). “Low-level” = the mechanism (`EmailSender`, a DB, an HTTP client).

## The problem with depending on a detail

```
class NotificationService {
    EmailSender sender = new EmailSender();   // ← welded to email
    void notify(String m){ sender.sendEmail(m); }
}
```

Your core logic now **knows about email**. Want SMS? You edit the high-level class. Want a fake sender for a unit test? You can’t. The important module is hostage to a trivial one.

## Invert it

Put an **abstraction** (`Sender`) between them. The high-level module depends on the interface; the low-level classes **implement** it:

```
class NotificationService {
    Sender sender;                       // an abstraction
    NotificationService(Sender s){ sender = s; }   // injected
    void notify(String m){ sender.send(m); }
}
```

The source-code dependency from `EmailSender` now points **UP** at the abstraction, it *inverted*. That’s the name. `NotificationService` no longer knows email exists; pass it an `SmsSender`, a `MockSender`, anything.

> This is the backbone of **dependency injection** and testable architecture. The interface is usually owned by the high-level module, the detail conforms to the policy, not the other way around.c:["$","div",null,{"className":"flex min-h-screen","children":[["$","$L17",null,{"activeModule":"lld-solid","activeLesson":"dependency-inversion"}],["$","$L18","dependency-inversion",{"lesson":{"id":"dependency-inversion","patternId":"lld-solid","title":"Dependency Inversion (D)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"Core","statement":"High-level policy shouldn’t depend on low-level details. Point BOTH at an abstraction, and watch the dependency arrow flip.","companies":["Amazon","Netflix","Uber","Microsoft"],"theory":"$19","approaches":[{"label":"Depending on a detail","code":["class NotificationService {","    EmailSender sender = new EmailSender();   // concrete","    void notify(String m){ sender.sendEmail(m); }","}"],"steps":[{"view":"class","codeLine":1,"narration":"NotificationService is the high-level policy, it decides WHEN to notify.","classDiagram":{"classes":[{"id":"svc","name":"NotifService","fields":["- sender: EmailSender"],"methods":["+ notify(msg)"],"x":0.4,"y":0.2},{"id":"email","name":"EmailSender","methods":["+ sendEmail(msg)"],"x":0.6,"y":1.7,"tint":"red"}],"relations":[{"id":"d","from":"svc","to":"email","kind":"depend","label":"new EmailSender()"}],"shownClassIds":["svc"],"shownRelIds":[],"activeIds":["svc"]}},{"view":"class","codeLine":2,"narration":"But it `new`s an EmailSender directly, so the arrow of dependency points straight DOWN at a concrete detail. The policy now knows about email plumbing.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["svc","email"],"shownRelIds":["d"],"activeIds":["email"]},"vars":[["depends on","a concrete class"]]},{"view":"class","narration":"Want SMS instead? Need a fake for a test? You must EDIT NotificationService. The important class is chained to the trivial one, exactly backwards.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["svc","email"],"shownRelIds":["d"],"activeIds":["svc","email"]},"vars":[["swap sender","must edit policy 💥"]]}]},{"label":"Invert with an abstraction","code":["interface Sender { void send(String m); }","class EmailSender implements Sender { ... }","class SmsSender   implements Sender { ... }","","class NotificationService {","    Sender sender;","    NotificationService(Sender s){ this.sender = s; }   // injected","}"],"steps":[{"view":"class","codeLine":1,"narration":"Introduce an abstraction, a Sender interface with just send(). The high-level module will depend on THIS, not on a concrete class.","classDiagram":{"classes":[{"id":"svc","name":"NotifService","fields":["- sender: Sender"],"methods":["+ notify(msg)"],"x":0,"y":0.2},{"id":"iface","name":"Sender","stereotype":"interface","methods":["+ send(msg)"],"x":1.5,"y":0.95,"tint":"accent"},{"id":"email","name":"EmailSender","methods":["+ send(msg)"],"x":0.7,"y":1.9},{"id":"sms","name":"SmsSender","methods":["+ send(msg)"],"x":2.1,"y":1.9}],"relations":[{"id":"hi","from":"svc","to":"iface","kind":"depend","label":"depends on"},{"id":"e","from":"email","to":"iface","kind":"implement"},{"id":"s","from":"sms","to":"iface","kind":"implement"}],"shownClassIds":["svc","iface"],"shownRelIds":["hi"],"activeIds":["iface"]},"vars":[["depends on","an abstraction"]]},{"view":"class","codeLine":2,"narration":"EmailSender implements Sender, so its dependency arrow now points UP at the interface. The low-level detail just inverted its direction. That’s the inversion.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["svc","iface","email"],"shownRelIds":["hi","e"],"activeIds":["email"]},"vars":[["EmailSender →","now points UP ⤴"]]},{"view":"class","codeLine":3,"narration":"Add SmsSender the same way. NotificationService never changes, inject whichever sender you like, including a mock in tests.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["svc","iface","email","sms"],"shownRelIds":["hi","e","s"],"activeIds":["sms"]}},{"view":"class","narration":"Both modules now depend on the abstraction in the middle; neither depends on the other’s details. This is the spine of dependency injection and testable design.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["svc","iface","email","sms"],"shownRelIds":["hi","e","s"],"activeIds":["iface"]},"vars":[["swap sender","zero edits ✓"]]}]}]}}]]}]
